<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Les Cayes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Les Cayes is a port city on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, capital of the Sud <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. In the colonial and revolutionary periods it was the main town of the southern peninsula and the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">André Rigaud</a>, who based his separate southern government there in 1810–1811. In 1816 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> sailed from Les Cayes with Haitian help to renew the struggle for Venezuelan independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/les-cayes.jpg" alt="Les Cayes" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cathedral_Les_Cayes_Haiti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Les Cayes, often called Aux Cayes and in Haitian Creole Okay, is a commune and seaport in the Les Cayes Arrondissement in the Sud department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, with a population of 151,696. Isolated from the turmoil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, it is one of Haiti's major ports, exporting mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a>, with minor exports of bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>. It is the world's largest supplier of vetiver, a perfume ingredient, of which it exports 250 tons a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first European settlement in the south-west was Salvatierra de la Sabana, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a> in 1504; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Vasco Núñez de Balboa</a>, a co-founder, lived there for several years raising pigs before leaving hidden in a barrel on an expedition. The settlement was abandoned in 1540. The French colonial administration later founded Aux Cayes, named for its nearness to Île-à-Vache. Hurricanes destroyed the town in 1781 and 1788, and in July 1793 the whites of Les Cayes were massacred.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> arrived in Les Cayes on 24 December 1815 and on 2 January 1816 was introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a>, President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. After Bolívar pledged to free every slave in the areas he occupied, Pétion gave him money and military supplies. During the American occupation, planes bombed Les Cayes on 5 December 1919; the anthropologist Jean-Philippe Belleau called it possibly the first air attack on civilians. On 6 December 1929 U.S. Marines fired on about 1,500 Haitians protesting local economic conditions, killing 12 to 22 and wounding 51. The massacre provoked international outrage, and President Herbert Hoover called for an investigation of conditions in Haiti the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hurricane Matthew struck Les Cayes on 4 October 2016, and a magnitude 7.2 earthquake on 14 August 2021 near the city killed over 2,200 people and injured about 12,700, most of them in and around Les Cayes. The town draws visitors to Gelée Beach, the Botanical Garden at Bergeau and the nearby Île à Vache. It has an airport, Antoine-Simon Airport, and the public Hôpital Immaculée Conception. André Rigaud, the Haitian revolutionary leader, was born there on a plantation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Les Cayes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coffee remained the main export, shipped from Port-au-Prince, Jacmel and <b>Les Cayes</b> to European and American merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Discontent with Alexandre Pétion's leadership in the south allowed him to set up a separate government based at <b>Les Cayes</b> in November 1810.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-south-haiti">State of South Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
